"Two cars: car B keeps one constant acceleration. The meeting times solve x_A(t) = x_B(t) exactly, a quadratic within each stretch where both accelerations are constant.",
"Stopping distance: each car keeps its speed v for the reaction time t_r, then brakes with a constant deceleration b to a stop, so d = v t_r + v²/2b, computed as three stages of constant acceleration (coast, brake, stand). The opening t_r = {#} s and b = {#} m/s² are close to the UK Highway Code's typical stopping distances (rule {#}: thinking distance {#} m per mph; braking {#} m at {#} mph to {#} m at {#} mph, {#} to {#} m/s²). Real braking builds up over a fraction of a second, and the road is taken as level.",
"Ball on ramps: a solid ball rolling without slipping (I = 2mr²/{#}), so along a piece of slope θ, a = −({#}/{#}) g sin θ, with g = {#} m/s². No rolling resistance, and the ball keeps its speed through each bend (a real ball loses a little there). x is the distance along the track; each piece is {#} m long, and a stop at each end ends the run."
